      "description": "Phodilus — a genus of barn owls & bay owls in the family Tytonidae.",
      "lineage": "Phodilus",
      "children": [
        {
          "id": "PHODILUS_ASSIMILIS",
          "name": "Phodilus assimilis",
          "rank": "SPECIES",
          "commonName": "Sri Lanka Bay Owl",
          "lineage": "Phodilus",
          "continents": [
            "Europe"
          ],
          "subspeciesCount": 0,
          "description": "The Sri Lanka bay owl (Phodilus assimilis) is a species of bay owl in the family Tytonidae. It is endemic to the island of Sri Lanka and the Western Ghats in Kerala, South Western India. It was considered a subspecies of the Oriental bay owl (Phodilus badius) but is now treated as a full species due to its distinctive call, plumage and disjunct distribution.\nIts natural habitats are subtropical or tropical moist montane forests and subtropical or tropical high-altitude grassland. It is threatened by habitat loss.",
          "sourcedFrom": "gbif",
          "wikipediaTitle": "Sri Lanka bay owl",
          "children": [],
          "uuid": "b4510b75-5728-5ba-865-838de96917d437",
          "sourceIds": {
            "wikidata": [
              "Q2315214"
            ],
            "itis": [
              "1063231"
            ],
            "col": [
              "6VBY6"
            ],
            "checklistbank": "ott3595785",
            "wikispecies": "Phodilus_assimilis",
            "gbif-backbone": "6085600",
            "checklistbank-1174": "txn:402387",
            "checklistbank-139831": "144585"
          },
          "authorityIds": "col:6VBY6,itis:1063231,wikidata:Q2315214",
          "authorship": "Hume, 1877"
        },
        {
          "id": "PHODILUS_BADIUS",
          "name": "Phodilus badius",
          "rank": "SPECIES",
          "commonName": "bay owl",
          "lineage": "Phodilus",
          "continents": [
            "Europe"
          ],
          "subspeciesCount": 0,
          "description": "The Oriental bay owl (Phodilus badius) is a species of bay owl. It is completely nocturnal, and can be found throughout Southeast Asia and parts of India. It has several subspecies. It has a heart-shaped face with earlike extensions. The Congo bay owl (Phodilus prigoginei) was formerly classified as a subspecies of Oriental bay owl due to insufficient knowledge, but it has turned out that it might not even belong to the same genus. The Sri Lanka bay owl was also considered a subspecies.\nA population of this species has apparently become extinct on Samar Island in the Philippines during the 20th century. It was described as Phodilus badius riverae and was only ever known from a single specimen, which was lost in a bombing raid in 1945. The validity of this taxon is uncertain; it is usually synonymized with the nominate subspecies (for reasons of biogeography) or the subspecies saturatus (from external appearance); it might have been a distinct species, however.",
          "sourcedFrom": "gbif",
          "wikipediaTitle": "Oriental bay owl",
          "children": [],
          "uuid": "913bebf5-e523-53a-808-dde09d2a84cd3e",
          "sourceIds": {
